What actually happens (in your current system)

1. GET

• User → Server → Response

• Browser sends request:
  • GET /data

• FastAPI:
  • Finds matching route
  • Runs Python function

• Function:
  • Reads data from `items` (in memory)

• Response:
  • Data returned as JSON
  • Browser displays JSON

• Important:
  • No HTML processing
  • No “rendering” step
  • Just JSON over HTTP

--------------------------------------------------

2. POST

• User → Server → Store data → Response

• Client sends:
  • JSON body to /data

• FastAPI:
  • Validates input using Item model

• Function:
  • Appends data to `items`

• Server response:
  • Confirmation JSON returned

--------------------------------------------------

3. PUT

• User → Server → Modify existing data → Response

• Client sends:
  • ID + updated JSON

• FastAPI:
  • Finds item with matching ID in `items`

• Function:
  • Replaces existing data

• Response:
  • Updated item returned

--------------------------------------------------

4. DELETE

• User → Server → Remove data → Response

• Client sends:
  • ID in URL (/data/1)

• FastAPI:
  • Locates item in `items`

• Function:
  • Removes item from memory

• Response:
  • Success or not-found message

--------------------------------------------------

Correct simplified flow (mental model)

• Request
  → FastAPI route matching
  → Python function execution
  → In-memory data operation (items)
  → JSON response returned

Key idea:
• FastAPI = traffic controller
• Python function = logic
• `items` = temporary memory store
• JSON = communication format

--------------------------------------------------

One-line intuition
  GET    → reads data
  POST   → adds data
  PUT    → updates data
  DELETE → removes data
